OpenBSD's open_wmemstream, which Bionic carries, was wrong three ways:
- its string counted BUFSIZ * 4 wide characters in BUFSIZ * 4 bytes, so a
  write past the first BUFSIZ characters went out of the allocation;
- a write's limits were swapped: it took as many bytes as the string had
  room for, and made at most as many characters as it was given bytes,
  so it read past what it was given;
- a write answered the characters it made, not the bytes it took, so the
  stream wrote again what was left wherever a character had more bytes
  than one.
Now the string is counted and allocated in wide characters, and a write
converts the bytes it is given, a null character among them, and answers
them all.

--- a/libc/upstream-openbsd/lib/libc/stdio/open_wmemstream.c
+++ b/libc/upstream-openbsd/lib/libc/stdio/open_wmemstream.c
@@ -42,7 +42,8 @@
